Given an integer array nums, find the subarray with the largest sum, and return its sum.
Example:
Input: nums = [-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]
Output: 6
Explanation: The subarray [4,-1,2,1] has the largest sum 6.

Brute Force Approach
---------------------

public class MaximumSubarray {
	public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {
        int maxSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        // Outer loop marks the starting index of the subarray
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int currentSum = 0;
            
            // Inner loop marks the ending index and accumulates the sum
            for (int j = i; j < nums.length; j++) {
                currentSum += nums[j];
                
                // Update the maximum sum found so far
                if (currentSum > maxSum) {
                    maxSum = currentSum;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return maxSum;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		MaximumSubarray solution = new MaximumSubarray();
		int[] nums = { -2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4 };
		int maxSum = solution.maxSubArray(nums);
		System.out.println("Maximum subarray sum: " + maxSum); // Output: Maximum subarray sum: 6
	}
}

Time Complexity: O(n^2), where n is the number of elements in the input array. The outer loop runs n times, and for each iteration of the outer loop, the inner loop can run up to n times in the worst case, leading to a quadratic time complexity.
Space Complexity: O(1), as we are using a constant amount of extra space regardless of the input size. The variables maxSum and currentSum do not depend on the size of the input array.

Kadane's Algorithm
--------------------

public class MaximumSubarray {
	public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {
		int currentSum = nums[0];
        int maxSum = nums[0];

        // Start from index 1 since currentSum/maxSum already include nums[0]
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            // Either extend the previous subarray, or start fresh at nums[i]
            currentSum = Math.max(nums[i], currentSum + nums[i]);

            // Track the best sum seen across all positions
            maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, currentSum);
        }

        return maxSum;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		MaximumSubarray solution = new MaximumSubarray();
		int[] nums = { -2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4 };
		int maxSum = solution.maxSubArray(nums);
		System.out.println("Maximum subarray sum: " + maxSum); // Output: Maximum subarray sum: 6
	}
}

Time Complexity: O(n), where n is the number of elements in the input array. We iterate through the array once, performing constant time operations for each element.
Space Complexity: O(1), as we are using a fixed amount of space regardless of the input size.

Explanation - 
currentSum = Math.max(nums[i], currentSum + nums[i]);
If currentSum (the running total up to the previous index) is positive, adding it helps — so currentSum + nums[i] wins.
If currentSum is negative, it's dragging you down — you're better off abandoning it and starting fresh with just nums[i] alone.

nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]

i=0: currentSum = -2, maxSum = -2  (initial)
i=1: currentSum = max(1, -2+1=-1) = 1     maxSum = max(-2,1) = 1
i=2: currentSum = max(-3, 1-3=-2) = -2    maxSum = max(1,-2) = 1
i=3: currentSum = max(4, -2+4=2) = 4      maxSum = max(1,4) = 4
i=4: currentSum = max(-1, 4-1=3) = 3      maxSum = max(4,3) = 4
i=5: currentSum = max(2, 3+2=5) = 5       maxSum = max(4,5) = 5
i=6: currentSum = max(1, 5+1=6) = 6       maxSum = max(5,6) = 6
i=7: currentSum = max(-5, 6-5=1) = 1      maxSum = max(6,1) = 6
i=8: currentSum = max(4, 1+4=5) = 5       maxSum = max(6,5) = 6

Result: 6 (matches [4,-1,2,1])
